.TH FULL 4 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
full \- always full device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/full
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B full
device is a character device that mimics a full disk.
.PP
Any read operation to
.B /dev/full
will succeed and return a buffer filled with zero-valued bytes (0x00).
This behavior is identical to
.BR zero (4).
.PP
Any write operation to
.B /dev/full
will fail with the error
.B ENOSPC
(No space left on device).
This applies to
.BR write (2),
.BR pwrite (2),
.BR writev (2),
and any other function that attempts to write data to the device.
.PP
The device supports
.BR lseek (2)
which allows seeking to arbitrary offsets; however, the read/write behavior remains unchanged regardless of offset.
.PP
The device supports
.BR poll (2)
and indicates that it is always readable
.RB ( POLLIN )
and writable
.RB ( POLLOUT ).
Note that while it is "writable" in the sense that a write operation will not block, the write operation will immediately fail.
.PP
Attempts to map the device using
.BR mmap (2)
will fail.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/full
The full device special file (character device, major 1, minor 7).
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOSPC
Returned for all write operations.
.TP
.B ENOTTY
Returned for any
.BR ioctl (2)
operation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR null (4),
.BR zero (4),
.BR intro (4)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B full
device appeared in 4.3BSD-Reno.
.SH AUTHORS
This implementation was written for the Substrate Kernel.
